Reintroduce `azuread_application_app_role` / decouple `required_resource_access` under `azuread_application`?

### Description

Currently in a situation where `required_resource_access` in `azuread_application` prevents creation of let's say apps A, B, C with a, b, c roles and having all apps cross-reference each other circularly. 

Afaik current dynamic solutions would rely on monkey patching... Which is not ideal.

So is it possible to have decoupled resource `required_resource_access` under `azuread_application`? 

With a separate applications' required_resource one can create a batch of apps and then cross-link them totally at most. 

### New or Affected Resource(s)
* something new like `azuread_application_required_resource`
* bring back old `azuread_application_app_role` if it's related?

### Potential Terraform Configuration
```terraform
resource "azuread_application" "apps" {
   for_each = { ... }
   ...
}

resource "azuread_application_required_resource" "apps-resources" {
   depends_on = [ azuread_application.apps ]
   for_each = { ... }
   app_id = ...
   resource_app_id = ...
   resource_access {
      id   = ...
      type = ...
    }
}
```

azuread_service_principal not properly provisioned before running its dependencies

I am trying to create an application with a service principal using the following config:

```
resource "azuread_application" "main" {
  display_name    = "main-reader"
  sign_in_audience = "AzureADMyOrg"
  fallback_public_client_enabled = true
  
  web {
    implicit_grant {
      access_token_issuance_enabled = true
      id_token_issuance_enabled     = true
    }
  }
}

resource "azuread_service_principal" "main" {
  application_id = "${azuread_application.main.application_id}"
  
  provisioner "local-exec" {
       #the following command is used to grant admin consent for certain permissions
	command = "az ad app permission grant --id ${azuread_service_principal.main.application_id} --api 00000003-0000-0000-c000-000000000000 --scope 'User.Read.All'" #this command fails
	#command = "sleep 60;az ad app permission grant --id ${azuread_service_principal.main.application_id} --api 00000003-0000-0000-c000-000000000000 --scope 'User.Read.All'" #this command runs smoothly

	interpreter = ["powershell", "-c"]
  }
}
```

But while trying to provision this configuration i get the error:
```
│ Error running command 'az ad app permission grant --id 602e4230-8e6b-4cdb-8daf-da9a097d2055 --api 00000003-0000-0000-c000-000000000000 --scope 'User.Read.All'': exit status 1. Output:
│ ERROR: Service principal with appId or objectId '602e4230-8e6b-4cdb-8daf-da9a097d2055' doesn't exist. If '602e4230-8e6b-4cdb-8daf-da9a097d2055' is an appId, make sure an associated
│ service principal is created for the app. To create one, run `az ad sp create --id 602e4230-8e6b-4cdb-8daf-da9a097d2055`
```

But if i add a delay, e.g. sleep 60 before trying to grant a permission then it executes correctly.
It looks like the azuread_service_principal returns prematurely. I have also tried using a null_resource with dependency on the service principal but i had the same result.

